WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:03.880
Hey Crazies.

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:09.000
In the last video, we were talking about cosmological
redshift and, at the end, I said this:

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.180
Unfortunately, to know there’s even been
a redshift,

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.820
we have to know what color the light is supposed
to be.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:16.820
But that’s a topic for a another video.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:19.520
I was gonna wait, but a bunch of you asked
about it.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:20.940
So wait no longer!

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:22.300
This is that video!

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:26.300
But we can’t address this issue without
a deeper dive into the light from stars.

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.760
I mean, not literally, that would be bad.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.100
I just mean that light can tell us a lot!

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:42.840
We saw in a previous video that the color
of a star is only dependent on one thing:

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:44.320
Its surface temperature.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:49.020
If we combine that with the star’s brightness,
we can tell what type of star is it and how

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:50.380
long it’s been around.

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:54.460
Unfortunately, getting anything else out of
that light is going to take a lot more work.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:55.800
Let’s focus on our Sun.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.440
It’s a yellow-white dwarf, but it’s mostly
white.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:03.280
It only looks yellow to us on the ground because
the atmosphere scatters away some of the colors.

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:06.780
Astronauts, outside the atmosphere, see a
white Sun.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:10.140
So, the Sun is white, but white isn’t really
a wavelength of light.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.300
It’s what you get when you combine all the
colors together.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:13.820
What kinds of colors?

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:15.280
These kinds of colors!!

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:16.920
The ones in the visible spectrum.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:17.780
Side Note!

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:22.420
In the last video, I made the spectrum look
like this, except this magenta shouldn’t

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:24.520
be there because it doesn’t really exist.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.120
Thank you, Alberto Torres, for keeping us
accurate.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.160
End of side note!

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.620
If all these colors come from the same place,
it’s not very useful to see them all separate,

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:35.800
so our brain smudges it all together and calls
it white.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:39.720
The point is: Some of the colors we see are
combinations of other colors.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:42.180
It’s our brain trying to figure it all out.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:45.020
Didn’t you just say a star’s color only
depends on temperature?

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:47.140
Yeah, that’s a smidge of a lie though.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.640
It’s a bit more complicated.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.500
While it is true that most light coming from
a star is because of its temperature

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.140
a little tiny bit of it is controlled by a
couple other things.

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:59.540
Because of its temperature, the Sun emits
ALL wavelengths of light.

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:01.520
Including wavelengths that aren’t visible.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:02.340
However!

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.560
It doesn’t emit them all with the same brightness.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.000
As you can see, most of the light coming from
the Sun is in the visible range.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.080
and it peaks around yellowish-green.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:14.140
So this spectrum is not what the Sun’s light
looks like.

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:17.200
It’s more like this.
and that’s just the light from the particles

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:18.480
bumping into each other.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.240
The light due to the Sun’s temperature.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.860
But if you take a look at the actual colors
coming from the Sun, it looks like this.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:26.780
What?!?

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:28.320
Why are there missing colors?

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.560
They got absorbed as they were leaving the
Sun.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:34.810
They’re called “Absorption Lines” or
in the case of the Sun: Fraunhofer Lines

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:38.560
named after Joseph von Fraunhofer, the physicist
who first saw them.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.260
after looking through one of these.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:43.560
Well, he didn’t really look through it.
He shined the light through it.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.200
Don’t ever look directly at the Sun!

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:49.739
Anyway, each one of those dark lines comes
as part of a set and each set corresponds

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:51.540
to a specific type of atom.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:53.040
These are for Hydrogen.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.800
These are for Helium.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:56.500
and there are a ton of others.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:00.560
We know which atom goes with which set of
lines because we can create those same lines

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:01.600
here in a lab.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:02.900
Not through absorption though.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:07.320
If we get atoms excited, they’ll emit the
same colors they can absorb.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.680
These are the emission lines for hydrogen.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:13.880
You can see they line up perfectly with 4
of the absorption lines from the Sun.

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:17.620
We can also do this for Helium or any other
atom we’re looking for in the Sun.

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:19.120
It’s like an atomic fingerprint

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:22.700
except perfectly unique, 
so it's nothing like a fingerprint

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:24.160
It’s like Atomic DNA!

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:27.260
Using it, we can figure out what the outside
of the Sun is made of

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:31.480
and, through plasma and nuclear physics, infer
what the whole Sun is made of.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:33.040
We can do this for all different types of
stars

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.760
and get a good idea of what to expect for
each one.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.159
If we look at a star expecting to see this,
but, instead, see this then we know it is

00:03:40.159 --> 00:03:41.170
redshifted.

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:45.240
We know the star is moving toward us or away
from us and how fast it’s going

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.130
but it took a bunch of physics to get there.

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:50.680
So knowing how complicated this is, does it
make it more interesting or does it make it

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:51.500
scarier?

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:53.100
“Both” is an acceptable answer.

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:55.160
I’d like to hear your thoughts in the comments.

